  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWe had another day of record breaking temperatures as this extremely \nabnormally warm airmass sticks with us. MSP, STC, and EAU all set \ndaily high temperature records and MSP's 90 is the latest 90 \nrecorded in the calendar year. The stalled cold front is currently \ndraped from near Hinckley south-southwest along the northward moving \nMinnesota River to Fairmont. West of the front, temperatures cool \nfrom the lower 60s to lower 50s in western MN. Some patchy fog is \nbeing observed in our far western MN counties where temperatures \nhave cooled to the dewpoint. East of the front, it is stuffy with \nstill mid to even upper 70s over southeastern MN and WI at this \nearly hour. The front will stay in place for most of today, allowing \ntemperatures get very warm to its east. Low to mid 80s are forecast \nfrom southern MN into western WI. EAU's high of 87 would break the \ndaily record so another record breaking day of heat is likely. Highs \ncool to the west with western MN forecast in the mid to upper 60s. \nAs we near midday, a few CAMs show a broken line of convection \ndeveloping in western MN and moving northeast as synoptic lift \nincreases over the surface front. Have bumped up PoPs to 40-50% from \nwestern to central MN Monday afternoon for scattered showers and \neven a thunderstorm. As synoptic lift increases, a surface low will \ndevelop over southern MN Monday afternoon and travel northeast. More \nrain will develop well to our north initially, but activity \nwill spread south into our northern WI counties as the low exits\neast Monday night. 0.1\" to perhaps 0.25\" of rain is possible \nwith this activity.\n\nThe cold front moves east Monday night cooling us down to more \nnormal temperatures for this time of year. We will also enter a \nbrief dry period through mid-week. Tuesday's highs are forecast to \nbe in the mid to upper 50s in western and central MN to mid to upper \n60s for areas south and east. By Wednesday, highs will be a bit \ncooler with mid 50s to mid 60s expected across the CWA. Meanwhile, \ncentral MN should see some upper 30s for lows return by Wednesday \nnight. Guidance shows our next chance of rain arriving early \nThursday as forecast models show a cutoff low over the Rockies \ntraveling east into the Central Plains by late Thursday. Meanwhile, \nan upper-level trough within the northern jetstream is forecast to \ndig east-southeast from western Canada into the Northern Plains. \nGuidance doesn't favor the phasing of these 2 features until over \nthe Great Lakes Region, but a surface low should get going over the \nMidwest the second half of Thursday before traveling east. Model \nconfidence has increased in this solution such that 50-70% PoPs \nstretch from southern MN into WI during Thursday. We'll have to see \nhow far north precipitation reaches, though confidence in the \nnorthern portions of our CWA seeing meaningful rain has trended \ndownward. The northern stream trough is forecast to amplify as it \nmoves over the Great Lakes, causing enhanced northerly flow and CAA \nin its wake over the Upper Midwest. A majority of our CWA could see \nits first sub-freezing lows Saturday morning as a portion of an \narctic airmass advects into the region. However, this \"cold snap\" \nwill be short-lived as long-range guidance shows amplified upper-\nlevel ridging progressing east of the Rockies to start next weekend. \nThis feature would bring another round of likely dry weather and \nabove normal temperatures next weekend into early next week.",
